  spent a lot of time with him many many  Saturdays etc which is a Jewish Sabbath  I'd never seen him do that kind of thing  before I know he had strong Jewish  identity was very proud of his Judaism  and but I'd never seen him do anything  like that before so I we very quietly as  quite as I can be asked him about it on  the way to the thing and and he told me  a story he said that at the end of the  war they were death marching the  concentration camp inmates and  condensing them into various camps and  then they would destroy the old camps  and there's no evidence of war crimes  and in the last camp he was in I'm not  sure this time I think it was Turk i'm  i'm not sure near Dachau and they had  marched him into the camp but there were  different inmates from various camps now  and one day one morning he gets up and  he looked server and someone had  smuggled in a set of tefillin the Jewish  prayer boxes that Orthodox Jews use when  they do their morning and evening  prayers during a week and he saw this  man get up and say the prayers and put  them to fill in and when he was done dr.  Franco asked the man if he could borrow  his tefillin to put them on he just had  an urge to do this and he said I hadn't  put on tefillin since my bar mitzvah  when I learned how to do it but I just  felt like I want to do it and he asked  the man he had to kind of remind him how  to put it on and he put it on and he  said he couldn't he was so weak he  couldn't remember any Jewish prayers so  the only thing he could remember was the  cottage which is a prayer that affirms  the importance of life at a time when  people die people commonly refer to as a  prayer for the dead but it's really a  very life-affirming prayer and it's the  only thing but I'll Jews nor this prayer  and he's only thing he could remember by  heart so he got up put on tefillin that  day and said cottage well if you talk  about divine intervention that afternoon  the camp was liberated by american  soldiers and he told me that for the  rest of his life he got up every morning  put on tefillin and said Coty's so that  was the kind of spirituality beyond just  really  jessore or observant that he possessed  and I had never known that and I was  very private part of his life I'm almost  afraid to private to tell but it was  such a fascinating part of him and in  fact many years later when he was  getting old now you have to remember us  getting old I didn't meet him till he  was over 70 it was about 71 I'm sorry  Beth 71 when I first met him and I never  thought I used to say to you I wish I'd  met him when he was 50 because you know  how old am i how long will i get to know  him of course he lived to be 92 and it  was wonderful but many years later when  he was getting older we had a  conversation one day my family in  baltimore's in the funeral home business  where the jewish funeral director in  baltimore and he was always fascinated  by this because he always had to  concerns about his death one was that he  couldn't be buried next to le because in  cemeteries in Europe you're buried in  religious sections and you don't bury  Catholics with Jews in the United States  we have many non-sectarian cemeteries  and he found that very interesting in  fact my understanding was that at one  time he even looked into whether there  were any in Austria and there weren't  not any and then he sort of came to this  realization that it didn't matter  whether he was buried next to her not  she would always be in his heart so he  that was interesting him but the other  thing was that because his family his  daughter and his grandchildren are  Catholic it always bothered him that he  didn't have a son who would say Kaddish  for him because it's a tradition in  Jewish life for a child to say cottage  on the anniversary of the parents death  and it's a very important tradition I  always do it for my father and  grandfather and and he was talking to me  and I said well I always do it for my 